<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colony of Natal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1843">1843</a>–<a href="/years/1910">1910</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Natal Colony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colony of Natal was a British colony on the south-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, annexed in 1843 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republic of Natalia was taken over, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg">Pietermaritzburg</a> and its port at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>. Bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thukela-river">Thukela River</a>, it developed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> economy using indentured Indian labour. In 1910 it became a province of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal.jpg" alt="Colony of Natal" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Natal_Colony_(1875%E2%80%931910).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1823 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-farewell">Francis Farewell</a>, a former naval lieutenant, and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> merchants formed a company to trade on the south-east coast. In August 1824 Farewell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-francis-fynn">Henry Francis Fynn</a> obtained from Shaka a document ceding land including the port of Natal, and Farewell declared the territory a British possession. In 1835 the settlers, about 50 in number, laid out a town and named it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Benjamin D'Urban</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> fleeing British rule in the Cape took control of the port in May 1838 and founded the Natalia Republic, which suffered from disorganised government and poor relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a>. In December 1841 Sir George Napier, governor of the Cape, announced that British forces would reoccupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Port Natal</a>. On 8 August 1843 the Natal volksraad agreed to British terms, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> trekked over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Natal was proclaimed a British colony in 1843, administered from the Cape, and in 1856 became a separate colony with a legislative council of sixteen. Settlers arrived from the United Kingdom in numbers around 1850, and John Colenso came as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1854. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">Sugar</a> was introduced from 1852; because Africans did not volunteer in sufficient numbers, the first Indian indentured labourers arrived in 1860, and by 1904 Indians outnumbered whites in the colony. Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>, who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a> in 1893, helped found the Natal Indian Congress in 1894.</p><p>Responsible government was established in 1893, with John Robinson as first premier, under an electoral law framed to keep all but a very few Africans from the vote. In 1873 colonial forces broke up the Hlubi people of Chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langalibalele">Langalibalele</a>, who had not enforced the registration of firearms; women and children were killed and survivors were apprenticed to colonists. Zululand was annexed to Natal in 1897. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> the Siege of Ladysmith lasted until 28 February 1900, and districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> were added to Natal.</p><p>In 1906 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambatha-rebellion">Bambatha Rebellion</a> broke out, ostensibly over a poll tax of £1, and it was suppressed by colonial forces. On 31 May 1910 the colony became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natal-province">Natal Province</a>, one of the founding provinces of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colony of Natal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Cape Colony, the <b>Natal Colony</b>, the Transvaal Colony and the Orange River Colony became its four provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His half-brother Mpande, with Boer support, overthrew him in 1840 and ruled for more than three decades, a period of relative stability in which the kingdom accepted the loss of land south of the Thukela to the <b>British Colony of Natal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
